  "description": {
   "content": [
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "On Dec 7, 2009, Egbert Gramsbergen wrote to fedora-commons-users:",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "I noticed something strange in the data storage of Fedora.",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "Objects and datastreams are stored in a directory tree mirroring time down to the level of minutes, the time being creation time for objects and modifiction time for versionable datastreams. This should enable the repository to be reconstructed from the files in case of an emergency. So far so good.",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "But in my case, the times as implied form the directory tree are exactly one hour ahead of the times in the metadata in foxml. So it seems that \"directory tree\" time is local time (CET in my case) and \"metadata\" time is UTC. So if I really have to reconstruct the repository from the files, Fedora has to know about the timezone in which the repository was made. It seems brittle to me, and unnecessary. Why not use the same time for both? Or do I miss something?",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "btw, I use Fedora 3.0.",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    }
   ],
